      "content": "thanks, i see where you're going with this. it feels a bit more grounded. \n\nbut i noticed something while looking at these. for the gang leader and the social worker, both are described as african american, but their \"vibes\" are kind of polar opposites in terms of power—one is dangerous/imposing and the other is soft/empathetic. i guess that's a common way to write them in shows, but for my game i'm trying to avoid that being the *only* way people see those groups. \n\nalso, for the lawyer and the cop, they're both white/italian, but one is \"high-powered\" and \"charismatic\" while the other is just \"slick/opportunistic.\" \n\ncan u help me refine these? i want to make sure i'm not accidentally playing into super predictable tropes where race and social status are too purely linked. like, can u give me a second version of these archetypes but swap the ethnicities around or mix up the social statuses so it feels more like a complex, layered city instead of just tropes? i want the \"successful\" and \"corrupt\" characters to be able to come from any background. thx",
